TITLE = "Opposing organic and inorganic carbon responses mask a net loss of soil carbon this century"
SHORT_TITLE = "Joint SOC–SIC vulnerability"
ONE_SENTENCE = "Carbonate gains offset organic carbon losses only at equilibrium; on century timescales, soils lose carbon."

STRUCTURED = [
("INTRODUCTION", "Soils hold the largest active terrestrial carbon pool, in two chemically distinct forms: soil organic carbon (SOC), derived from plant and microbial residues, and soil inorganic carbon (SIC), present mainly as pedogenic and lithogenic carbonates. Global assessments, national greenhouse-gas inventories, and soil-carbon policies are built almost exclusively on SOC, whereas SIC is usually treated as inert. A recent global synthesis showed that SIC is a pool of comparable size that is vulnerable to acidification. Because SOC and SIC respond to the same climatic gradients in opposite directions (wetter and colder conditions favor SOC accumulation but carbonate dissolution), assessing either pool alone can misstate how total soil carbon will respond to climate change and nitrogen use."),
("RATIONALE", "We built a joint, depth-resolved inventory of SOC, SIC, and the mineral-associated (MAOC) and particulate (POC) organic carbon fractions to 2 m depth. Quality-controlled WoSIS profiles (174,092 profiles) and 1005 harmonized fraction measurements were linked to climate, soil, terrain, and lithology covariates with gradient-boosted models that were evaluated by random 10-fold cross-validation. We then distinguished two questions that are often conflated: where the soil carbon system would settle under a future climate (equilibrium, space-for-time), and how much of that change can be realized by 2100 given the slow turnover of deep SOC, MAOC, and carbonates (time-limited response). Finally, we re-evaluated nitrogen-driven carbonate loss with a published empirical pH–nitrogen relation under alternative fertilizer trajectories."),
("RESULTS", "Global soils store 2492 Pg SOC and 1603 Pg SIC in the upper 2 m (4095 Pg in total), with SIC accounting for 39% of the total and 88% of SIC lying below 30 cm. Aridity controls the partitioning between the two pools mainly through soil pH: SIC occurrence follows a sigmoid function of pH with a midpoint at pH 7.10, and the probability of carbonate presence rises steeply above pH ≈ 6.6. Under SSP5-8.5, equilibrium projections show SOC losses of 246 Pg [90% confidence interval (CI), 157 to 318 Pg] nearly matched by SIC gains of 290 Pg (95 to 355 Pg), so that the net change in total carbon is indistinguishable from zero. Organic losses and inorganic gains co-occur on 42% of land. Under first-order turnover constraints, however, only ~3% of the equilibrium SIC gain is realized by 2100, whereas most of the SOC loss is, yielding a net loss of 182 Pg (119 to 266 Pg) of total soil carbon. Particulate organic carbon realizes almost all of its equilibrium loss (38 Pg in 0 to 30 cm) this century, while MAOC realizes 46 of its 111 Pg equilibrium loss and carries the larger committed loss beyond 2100. Nitrogen-driven topsoil carbonate loss for 2020–2050 is 1.8 to 2.5 Pg with IMAGE fertilizer projections, and nitrogen inputs 9- to 13-fold larger are needed to reproduce previously published losses of 10 to 23 Pg."),
("CONCLUSION", "Opposing SOC and SIC responses create an apparent equilibrium balance that does not hold on policy-relevant timescales: on the century scale, organic losses dominate and soils become a net carbon source. Carbon accounting that monitors only topsoil SOC misses most of the inorganic pool and the depth at which committed losses accumulate, and estimates of acidification-driven carbonate loss depend more on the assumed nitrogen trajectory than on the carbonate map itself. Joint SOC–SIC monitoring, depth-explicit inventories, and nitrogen and liming management that limit acidification should be part of how soils are represented in climate policy."),
]

ABSTRACT = "Soil organic carbon (SOC) and soil inorganic carbon (SIC) respond in opposite directions to climate, yet are rarely assessed together. Using 174,092 quality-controlled soil profiles and machine-learning models, we estimate that soils store 2492 Pg SOC and 1603 Pg SIC in the top 2 m. Under a high-emission scenario, equilibrium projections show SOC losses (246 Pg) nearly offset by SIC gains (290 Pg), but slow carbonate formation means that only a small fraction of these gains can be realized by 2100, giving a net loss of 182 Pg [90% confidence interval, 119 to 266 Pg]. Particulate organic carbon realizes its losses this century, whereas mineral-associated carbon carries a larger committed loss. Nitrogen-driven carbonate losses depend strongly on the fertilizer scenario."

# Main text: list of (kind, text); kind in {'h','p'}
MAIN = [
('p', "Soil carbon is the largest active carbon reservoir on land and a central term in the global carbon budget [@friedlingstein22;@lal04]. Almost all of what is known about its size and vulnerability, however, concerns soil organic carbon (SOC) [@batjes96;@sanderman17;@crowther16]. Soil inorganic carbon (SIC), mainly calcium and magnesium carbonates inherited from parent material or formed in situ through pedogenesis [@zamanian16;@monger15], has conventionally been regarded as a stable pool with turnover times of millennia. That view is changing. Carbonates dissolve within decades when soils acidify [@kim20;@song22], nitrogen fertilization accelerates carbonate-derived CO₂ release [@zamanian18;@raza20], and a recent global synthesis estimated that 2305 ± 636 Pg of SIC is stored in the top 2 m of soil, with up to 23 Pg of topsoil SIC at risk from nitrogen-driven acidification over the next 30 years [@huang24]."),
('p', "SOC and SIC are not independent. Both are governed by the soil water balance, which sets soil pH [@slessarev16]: leaching in humid climates removes base cations and carbonates while favoring plant inputs and organic matter accumulation, whereas in drylands low leaching allows carbonates to persist and organic inputs are small. The same climatic gradient therefore pushes the two pools in opposite directions. A warming and, in many regions, drying climate that depletes SOC may move soils toward conditions in which carbonates are thermodynamically favored. Whether such compensation is real on the timescales relevant to climate policy is unknown, because SOC can respond within decades [@crowther16;@koven17], whereas carbonate accumulation is slow [@zamanian16;@monger15]. Within SOC itself, particulate organic carbon (POC) turns over in years to decades, whereas mineral-associated organic carbon (MAOC) persists for decades to centuries [@lavallee20;@cotrufo19;@georgiou22]. Global assessments that treat SOC and SIC separately, or that equate an equilibrium shift with a realized flux, may therefore misstate the direction and timing of soil-carbon change."),
('p', "Here we provide a joint, depth-resolved inventory of SOC, SIC, MAOC and POC, and use it to ask three questions. First, how is soil carbon partitioned between organic and inorganic forms globally, and what controls that partitioning? Second, how will climate change redistribute carbon between the two pools at equilibrium, and how much of that redistribution can be realized by 2100? Third, how sensitive are estimates of nitrogen-driven carbonate loss to the nitrogen scenario? We follow the digital soil-mapping framework of Huang *et al.* [@huang24] where it defines current practice, including observation screening, two-step (occurrence and amount) models for SIC, random 10-fold cross-validation as the primary accuracy measure, and the empirical relation between nitrogen input and pH change, and we extend it with organic-horizon handling for SOC, declustering of dense survey networks, organic-carbon fractions, and an explicit time-limited response (materials and methods)."),

('h', "A joint inventory of soil organic and inorganic carbon"),
('p', "We compiled soil profiles from the World Soil Information Service (WoSIS) [@batjes20] and screened them following Huang *et al.* [@huang24] by removing litter and organic surface layers, depth-inconsistent horizons, samples collected before 1970, out-of-range values, and duplicates. Of 226,821 profiles, 174,092 were retained, including 107,482 with topsoil (0 to 30 cm) SOC, 99,199 with SIC, and 118,807 with pH (table S1). Profiles were harmonized to 0 to 30, 30 to 100, and 100 to 200 cm with mass-preserving splines [@bishop99;@malone09]. For each layer, we trained gradient-boosted models [@chen16] for pH; for SIC as a two-step model, first classifying carbonate presence (SIC > 0.1 g kg⁻¹) and then predicting the amount where present, with predicted pH as a covariate; for mineral-soil SOC, with a separate classifier for organic horizons constrained by the global peatland area [@xu18]; and for bulk density. Observations were weighted by the inverse number of profiles in each 0.5° cell to limit the influence of dense national survey networks (materials and methods)."),
('p', "Under random 10-fold cross-validation, the same protocol used by Huang *et al.* [@huang24], the models discriminated carbonate-bearing from carbonate-free soils well [area under the curve (AUC) = 0.95 to 0.97; F1 = 0.73 to 0.86 across depths] and predicted bulk density (R² = 0.77 to 0.81), topsoil mineral SOC (R² = 0.64), and pH (R² = 0.65 to 0.67) with moderate skill (table S3 and fig. S1). The amount of SIC where present was harder to predict (R² = 0.31 to 0.49 on the original scale; 0.41 to 0.62 on the log scale). These scores are below those reported by Huang *et al.* for SIC (AUC ≈ 0.99, R² ≈ 0.79) and pH (R² ≈ 0.85) [@huang24], a gap that we attribute to our smaller covariate set (no vegetation, drought-index, or topographic-wetness covariates), a coarser 10′ training stack, and differences in the observation population. We report this gap rather than switching to an alternative metric. Because random cross-validation is optimistic when observations are spatially clustered [@ploton20;@meyer21], all scores should be read as interpolation skill within the sampled environmental space."),
('p', "Global soils store 2492 Pg SOC and 1603 Pg SIC in the upper 2 m, or 4095 Pg of carbon in total (Fig. 1 and table S5). SIC therefore makes up 39% of total soil carbon. The two pools are distributed very differently with depth. SOC is spread almost evenly across the three layers (858, 826, and 808 Pg), whereas SIC increases strongly with depth (185, 614, and 804 Pg), so that 88% of SIC, but only 66% of SOC, lies below 30 cm (Fig. 1, A and B). Organic horizons in peatlands contribute 357 Pg (14%) of SOC. In the upper 1 m, our SIC estimate (800 Pg) lies within the range of earlier soil-unit-based estimates [695 to 748 Pg [@batjes96]; 940 Pg [@lal99]], and the 0 to 2 m total is close to the WISE30sec-based estimate of 1558 Pg [@plaza18] but 30% below the 2305 ± 636 Pg of Huang *et al.* [@huang24], just outside its ±1 SD range (table S6). Our topsoil SOC (858 Pg) is 20 to 25% higher than earlier inventories [684 to 724 Pg [@batjes96]; 658 Pg in SoilGrids on the same land mask [@poggio21]], and independent validation against 15,378 LUCAS topsoil samples [@orgiazzi18] confirms a positive bias (mean error +28.6 Mg C ha⁻¹; table S6). The 0 to 2 m SOC total (2492 Pg) agrees with the 2376 to 2456 Pg of Batjes [@batjes96] to within 5%."),
('p', "SIC and SOC occupy largely complementary territories (Figs. 1 and 2). SIC exceeds 60% of total soil carbon on 26% of land, including the Sahara, the Arabian Peninsula, central Asia, interior Australia, and the western interior of North America, whereas it contributes less than 5% on 44% of land, mainly in humid tropical and boreal regions (Fig. 2A). Russia, Canada, the United States, and China hold the largest total soil-carbon stocks, but their composition differs, and in several dryland countries, including Saudi Arabia, Iran, and Algeria, SIC is the dominant component of the national total (Fig. 2, B to D). Across countries, the SIC share is not predicted by the size of the SOC stock, and roughly a quarter of countries fall in each quadrant defined by the global medians (Fig. 2C). A national carbon inventory that tracks SOC alone therefore misses a large, and in some countries the dominant, part of the soil carbon stock."),
('p', "We produced global maps of present-day SOC, SIC, and pH at 30 arc sec (~1 km) by applying the same models to a hybrid covariate stack in which climate was interpolated from the 10′ grid and elevation was used at native resolution (fig. S12). The topsoil SOC integral at 30 arc sec (908 Pg) agrees with the 10′ value to within 6%, whereas the topsoil SIC integral is 44% lower (104 versus 185 Pg), indicating that SIC totals are sensitive to how the land mask and terrain are represented in carbonate-rich, topographically complex regions. We therefore use the 10′ grid, on which all models were trained and evaluated, for global totals and treat the 30 arc sec maps as a spatial product (materials and methods)."),

('h', "Aridity sets the partitioning of soil carbon through pH"),
('p', "Shapley additive explanations [@lundberg17;@lundberg20] show that the two pools are controlled by different groups of drivers (Fig. 7A and fig. S3). Climate accounts for 80% of the explained variation in pH and 66% in mineral SOC, with the logarithm of the humidity index ln(1 + P/PET), where P is precipitation and PET is potential evapotranspiration, as the leading predictor of both (26% and 14% of mean |SHAP|, respectively). In contrast, soil properties account for 74% of the explained variation in SIC presence and 55% in SIC amount, overwhelmingly through predicted pH (51% and 33%). Climate therefore affects carbonates mainly indirectly, by setting pH. A standardized path model fitted to 58,404 topsoil observations supports this interpretation (fig. S8): the humidity index has a strong negative effect on pH (β = −0.56), pH has a strong positive effect on SIC (β = 0.67), and the direct effect of the humidity index on SIC is negligible (β = 0.03), so that nearly all of the climate effect on SIC (−0.35 in total) is routed through pH (−0.38). The humidity index has, by contrast, a direct positive effect on SOC (β = 0.35). These linear paths are descriptive and do not replace the nonlinear model, but both analyses point to the same mechanism: water balance sets pH [@slessarev16], and pH sets whether carbonates can persist [@huang24]."),
('p', "The resulting relations are strongly nonlinear (Fig. 7, E to H, and figs. S4 to S6). In the observations, the probability of carbonate presence follows a logistic function of pH with a midpoint (pH₅₀) of 7.10 (n = 91,273 topsoil samples; fig. S6A), and the median SIC content of carbonate-bearing soils increases by nearly two orders of magnitude between pH 5 and 8.4 before leveling off (fig. S6B). The partial dependence of modeled carbonate presence on pH shows a breakpoint at pH 6.64, above which the probability of presence rises steeply (Fig. 7F). The breakpoint in the partial dependence of pH on the humidity index lies at ln(1 + P/PET) = 0.75 (P/PET ≈ 1.1), beyond which soils converge on an acidic plateau near pH 5.9 (fig. S5L). Holding pH constant, the residual partial dependence of carbonate presence on climate is weak (a change in probability of about 0.03), with breakpoints at P/PET ≈ 0.74 [ln(1 + P/PET) = 0.56] and a mean annual precipitation of 457 mm (Fig. 7E and fig. S5, A and B). The pH breakpoint near P/PET ≈ 1.1 coincides with the water-balance threshold at which precipitation begins to exceed evapotranspiration and soils acidify [@slessarev16], and the carbonate breakpoint at P/PET ≈ 0.74 lies close to the conventional boundary of drylands (aridity index 0.65). Our observed sigmoid describes carbonate occurrence and is centered at a lower pH than the curve reported by Huang *et al.* for the pH contribution to SIC amount, which changes most steeply between pH 7 and 8.5 [@huang24]; the two curves are complementary because carbonates first appear near neutral pH and then accumulate as pH approaches calcite equilibrium."),
('p', "Within SOC, the fraction held as MAOC (f_MAOC) is controlled more by texture than by climate. Clay content, silt content, and cation exchange capacity show breakpoints at 22%, 32%, and 25 cmol kg⁻¹, respectively (Fig. 7, G and H, and fig. S5, E to G), consistent with the mineral control on organic-matter protection [@rasmussen18;@georgiou22]. Mineral SOC declines steeply with increasing mean annual temperature up to a breakpoint at −3.8°C and more gradually above it (fig. S5J), in line with the higher temperature sensitivity of soil carbon in cold climates [@koven17]."),

('h', "Climate change shifts carbon from organic to inorganic pools at equilibrium"),
('p', "We projected equilibrium changes in SOC and SIC by applying the present-day models to 2081–2100 climates from six CMIP6 models under four shared socioeconomic pathways (SSPs) [@oneill16] while holding soil and terrain covariates fixed (space-for-time substitution). Uncertainty was propagated by combining bootstrap refits of the models with the six climate models; we report medians and 5th to 95th percentiles as 90% CIs. Under SSP5-8.5, equilibrium SOC in the upper 2 m declines by 246 Pg (90% CI, 157 to 318 Pg), whereas SIC increases by 290 Pg (95 to 355 Pg; fig. S13 and table S7). The net change in total soil carbon, +25 Pg (−106 to +109 Pg), is indistinguishable from zero. The same near-cancellation holds for all scenarios: under SSP1-2.6, SOC declines by 119 Pg (95 to 144 Pg) and SIC increases by 122 Pg (61 to 148 Pg), giving a net change of −1 Pg (−47 to +38 Pg)."),
('p', "The maps in Figs. 3 and 5 show the six-model mean change predicted by the central model fitted to all observations. Summed globally, these fields give equilibrium changes under SSP5-8.5 of −206 Pg SOC and +206 Pg SIC (0 to 2 m), with a range across climate models of −175 to −238 Pg for SOC. These central-model totals lie within the 90% CIs of the bootstrap ensemble but are smaller in magnitude than the ensemble medians because bootstrap refits extrapolate more strongly into novel climates (supplementary text S2). We use the ensemble medians and CIs for all headline values and the central-model fields for maps and spatial statistics, and we label the two accordingly throughout."),
('p', "SOC losses are widespread: equilibrium topsoil SOC declines on 70% of land under SSP1-2.6 and 80% under SSP5-8.5 (Fig. 3A), and losses are concentrated in boreal and cold-temperate regions of North America and Eurasia (Fig. 3, C to E). SIC gains are concentrated in Europe, northern and central Asia, and the western and central United States, whereas SIC declines in India, the Arabian Peninsula, the Sahel, the eastern United States, and the Canadian Prairies, where projected changes in water balance lower pH (Fig. 3F). This geography resembles the climate-driven pH responses of Huang *et al.*, who projected the largest topsoil SIC losses in Saudi Arabia and India and the largest gains in Russia, the United States, Spain, and France [@huang24]. Organic losses and inorganic gains co-occur on 42% of land under SSP5-8.5 (57 Mkm²; Fig. 5A and table S8), and 52% of countries fall in this trade-off quadrant (Fig. 5B). At the continental scale, Europe and North America account for most of the SOC loss, whereas Europe and Asia account for most of the SIC gain (Fig. 5C)."),
('p', "Shifts in Köppen–Geiger climate zones [@beck23] by 2071–2099 affect 7.2 Mkm² (5.6% of land) under SSP1-2.6 and 19.1 Mkm² (14.9%) under SSP5-8.5 (table S8). Under SSP5-8.5, land that changes climate zone accounts for 15% of the central-model SOC loss (−30 Pg) and 22% of the SIC gain (+45 Pg), roughly in proportion to its area for SOC but disproportionately for SIC. Most of the redistribution therefore occurs within existing climate zones, through gradual changes in water balance, rather than at shifting zone boundaries."),

('h', "Slow carbonate kinetics turn an equilibrium balance into a net loss"),
('p', "Space-for-time projections describe where the soil carbon system would settle if it fully equilibrated with a new climate. They do not describe how quickly it would get there. To translate equilibrium changes into changes realized by 2100, we applied a first-order response, Δ(t) = Δ_eq[1 − exp(−t/τ)], with t = 85 years and turnover times τ sampled from log-uniform ranges informed by radiocarbon and turnover syntheses [@carvalhais14;@he16;@shi20]: 20 to 100 years for SOC at 0 to 30 cm, 100 to 1000 years at 30 to 100 cm, 500 to 3000 years at 100 to 200 cm, and 1000 to 10,000 years for SIC at all depths (materials and methods). These ranges are assumptions; we chose them to bracket published estimates rather than to fit our data."),
('p', "Under these constraints, the equilibrium balance disappears (fig. S13 and table S7). By 2100 under SSP5-8.5, SOC declines by 190 Pg (127 to 277 Pg), or 77% of its equilibrium change, whereas SIC increases by only 8 Pg (−1 to +18 Pg), about 3% of its equilibrium gain. Total soil carbon therefore declines by 182 Pg (119 to 266 Pg). Net losses are robust in sign across all scenarios, ranging from 67 Pg (34 to 149 Pg) under SSP1-2.6 to 154 Pg (97 to 239 Pg) under SSP3-7.0, and the 90% CIs exclude zero in every case. Because turnover is fastest near the surface, 93% of the realized SOC loss under SSP5-8.5 occurs in the upper 30 cm (177 Pg; 115 to 262 Pg). The magnitude of these losses depends on the assumed τ, but their sign does not: even the lower bounds of the SIC turnover range realize only a small fraction of the equilibrium carbonate gain within the century."),
('p', "This asymmetry has a simple physical basis. Organic matter is lost through microbial respiration, which responds to temperature and moisture within years, whereas pedogenic carbonate accumulation requires a supply of calcium and magnesium from weathering or dust and the slow precipitation of carbonate in a drying profile [@zamanian16;@monger15]. Carbonate loss under acidification can be rapid [@kim20;@song22], but carbonate gain is slow. An equilibrium SIC gain is therefore a long-term potential, not a near-term sink, and it should not be credited against near-term SOC losses."),

('h', "Particulate carbon realizes the loss; mineral-associated carbon commits it"),
('p', "To resolve which organic pools carry these losses, we modeled the MAOC fraction of mineral-soil SOC from 1005 harmonized particle-size and density-fractionation measurements [@georgiou22;@georgiou_data] (fig. S2). After removing records in which POC was not measured, the model reached a random 10-fold cross-validated R² of 0.58 (five repeats, 0.57 ± 0.02; RMSE = 0.129; fig. S1B). In mineral soils, MAOC stores 525 Pg (90% CI, 508 to 538 Pg) and POC 187 Pg (173 to 203 Pg) in the upper 30 cm, and MAOC stores 1063 Pg in the upper 1 m (Fig. 6, A, C, D, and F), with an area-mean MAOC fraction of 0.72 in topsoils. Our 0 to 1 m MAOC estimate lies between the 899 Pg published by Georgiou *et al.* for non-permafrost mineral soils and the 1133 Pg obtained by summing their gridded product over all land [@georgiou22]. Subsoil estimates rest on only 34 fraction records below 30 cm and should be considered indicative."),
('p', "Under SSP5-8.5, equilibrium topsoil losses are 111 Pg for MAOC (76 to 158 Pg across climate models) and 38 Pg for POC (25 to 54 Pg) (Fig. 6B and table S10). With turnover times of 15 years for POC and 150 years for MAOC [@lavallee20;@cotrufo19], POC realizes essentially its entire equilibrium loss by 2100 (38 Pg), whereas MAOC realizes 46 Pg, or 41% (Fig. 6B). The remaining MAOC loss, about 65 Pg in the topsoil alone, is committed beyond 2100. This partitioning is sensitive to τ_MAOC: with τ = 50 or 400 years, the realized MAOC loss is 89 or 20 Pg, respectively, whereas POC losses change little (36 to 38 Pg; fig. S7). Losses are therefore expressed first in the fast, management-responsive POC pool, which is also the pool most directly targeted by practices that increase organic inputs, while the slow MAOC pool, often considered the most persistent store [@lavallee20;@georgiou22], carries a delayed loss that will continue to be realized after emissions are reduced. MAOC and POC are both below their global medians on 43% of land, mainly in drylands (Fig. 6E). These projections apply to mineral soils and are not directly additive with the SOC projections above, because they use a separate fraction model and exclude organic horizons (supplementary text S2)."),

('h', "Nitrogen-driven acidification: the nitrogen scenario dominates the estimate"),
('p', "Huang *et al.* estimated that nitrogen-induced acidification would remove 10, 14, and 23 Pg of topsoil SIC over 2020–2050 under three INMS nitrogen-policy scenarios projected with the GLOBIOM model [@huang24]. We applied the same empirical relation between cumulative fertilizer nitrogen input (N_in, Mg N ha⁻¹) and pH change derived from repeated Chinese soil surveys, ΔpH = −0.06 N_in + 0.11 [@huang24;@guo10], to cropland fertilizer projections from the IMAGE model distributed through ISIMIP3b [@stehfest14;@lange19], and converted ΔpH into ΔSIC through finite differences of our SIC model with all other covariates held fixed (materials and methods). Mean cumulative cropland inputs over 2020–2050 are 2.6 to 2.7 Mg N ha⁻¹ (~87 kg N ha⁻¹ year⁻¹) under SSP1-2.6, SSP3-7.0, and SSP5-8.5. Counting only cells that lose carbonate, these inputs remove 1.8, 2.4, and 2.5 Pg of topsoil SIC, respectively, and 14 to 18 Pg over 0 to 2 m (Fig. 4 and table S9). Losses are largest in the United States, China, India, and Pakistan, each losing about 2 to 4 Pg over 0 to 2 m under SSP5-8.5 (Fig. 4B)."),
('p', "Our topsoil estimates are 5.5 to 9 times smaller than those of Huang *et al.* Under the same ΔpH relation and our SIC response, reproducing their published magnitudes requires multiplying IMAGE cropland nitrogen inputs by factors of 12.7, 9.6, and 9.2 for their 10, 14, and 23 Pg estimates, respectively (table S9). Adding atmospheric deposition raises our topsoil losses by only 0.25 to 0.4 Pg, and a stoichiometric approach [0.21 kg C per kg N; range 0.055 to 0.32 [@zamanian18]] gives 0.8 to 0.9 Pg (range 0.2 to 1.3 Pg). The discrepancy therefore arises mainly from the nitrogen trajectory, not from differences in the carbonate map. The ΔpH relation was calibrated on intensively fertilized Chinese croplands [@guo10], and its positive intercept implies alkalinization below 1.8 Mg N ha⁻¹; applying it to low-input croplands elsewhere is an extrapolation. Global estimates of nitrogen-driven SIC loss should therefore be reported together with the nitrogen-input trajectory that produces them, preferably across several land-use models."),

('h', "Implications for carbon accounting and policy"),
('p', "Our results have three implications for how soils are represented in climate policy. First, the opposite responses of SOC and SIC to climate mean that assessments of either pool alone can be misleading in both directions. An SOC-only assessment overstates the equilibrium loss of total soil carbon, whereas an equilibrium assessment of both pools understates the loss over the coming century. Because carbonate gains are slow and most SOC losses are rapid, soils are projected to be a net carbon source this century under all four scenarios, adding to the climate–carbon feedbacks assessed by the IPCC [@canadell21]. Carbonate gains should not be counted as an offset for SOC losses in national inventories or in projections that inform nationally determined contributions (NDCs)."),
('p', "Second, inventory depth matters. The IPCC Tier 1 method for soil carbon stock change considers SOC in the upper 30 cm of mineral soils [@ipcc06;@ipcc19], which in our inventory contains 34% of SOC and 12% of SIC. Most of the inorganic pool, and the deep organic carbon that carries long-lived committed losses, lies outside this window. Inventories and soil-carbon monitoring, reporting, and verification schemes, including those supporting soil-carbon crediting [@minasny17], should report SIC and deeper layers at least in carbonate-bearing regions and should distinguish fast (POC) from slow (MAOC) organic pools."),
('p', "Third, nitrogen and acidity management affect both pools. IPCC inventory guidance accounts for CO₂ from applied agricultural lime [@ipcc06], but not for the dissolution of native carbonates driven by fertilizer acidification [@zamanian18;@raza20]. Reducing nitrogen surpluses limits this loss, and liming can counteract acidification, although its net CO₂ balance depends on whether dissolved carbonate is exported as bicarbonate or released as CO₂ [@hamilton07;@west05]. Similar considerations apply to enhanced rock weathering on croplands, which interacts with soil pH and carbonate equilibria [@beerling20]. Because the most vulnerable soils lie near the pH threshold we identified (pH ≈ 6.6 to 7.1), maps of that threshold could help target nitrogen and liming management where carbonate losses would otherwise be greatest."),
('p', "Our analysis has important limitations (supplementary text S6). The space-for-time approach assumes that present-day relationships between climate and soil carbon hold under future climates and ignores land-use change, irrigation, dust inputs, and direct effects of elevated CO₂ on soil pCO₂ and carbonate equilibria. The first-order time-limited response is a deliberately simple bridge between equilibrium maps and realized change, not a process model, and its τ values are assumptions. Our covariate set lacks vegetation and nitrogen-deposition predictors, so drivers acting through these pathways cannot be attributed. Random cross-validation overstates out-of-sample skill in sparsely sampled regions [@ploton20;@meyer21], topsoil SOC remains biased high against independent data, and our SIC total is lower than that of Huang *et al.* Present-day totals are reported as central estimates, and the future projections were computed with a preliminary generation of the same models that predates the final screening and declustering (supplementary text S2). Each of these caveats affects magnitudes. None changes the central asymmetry: carbonate gains are slow, organic losses are fast, and the apparent equilibrium balance between them does not hold this century."),
('p', "Joint mapping of SOC and SIC thus changes the question from how much carbon soils hold to how quickly each form responds. Answering that question will require repeated measurements of both pools at the same sites, better constraints on carbonate formation rates, and nitrogen scenarios evaluated across land-use models. Until then, the most defensible reading of the evidence is that soils will lose carbon over this century, and that the inorganic pool, though large, will not compensate for those losses on timescales relevant to climate policy."),
]

METHODS_SUMMARY = [
"Soil profiles from WoSIS [@batjes20] were screened following Huang *et al.* [@huang24] (table S1) and harmonized to 0–30, 30–100, and 100–200 cm with equal-area splines [@bishop99;@malone09]. SIC was computed as 0.12 × calcium carbonate equivalent. Thirty-three covariates (19 WorldClim bioclimatic variables [@fick17], Hargreaves PET [@hargreaves85] and ln(1 + P/PET), elevation, slope, topographic position, SoilGrids clay, sand, silt, and cation exchange capacity [@poggio21], and GLiM lithology [@hartmann12]) were assembled on a 10′ grid (table S2). For each layer, XGBoost [@chen16] models were trained for pH, SIC (presence classifier plus log-amount regression with smearing correction [@duan83] and predicted pH as covariate), mineral SOC with an organic-horizon classifier scaled to PEATMAP [@xu18], and bulk density, with 0.5° declustering weights. Hyperparameters were selected and accuracy assessed by random 10-fold cross-validation (tables S3 and S4). Stocks were computed as concentration × bulk density × (1 − coarse fraction) × thickness. MAOC fractions were modeled from 1005 measurements [@georgiou22] (table S12) with 50 bootstrap refits for 90% CIs. Future climates (2081–2100; six CMIP6 models; SSP1-2.6 to SSP5-8.5) were applied with the delta method; equilibrium changes combine bootstrap refits and climate models (median and 5th to 95th percentiles), and time-limited changes apply first-order responses with depth- and pool-specific τ ranges. Nitrogen-driven ΔpH followed ΔpH = −0.06 N_in + 0.11 [@huang24] with ISIMIP3b IMAGE fertilizer inputs. Drivers were attributed with TreeSHAP [@lundberg20], partial dependence with two-segment breakpoints, observed pH–SIC logistic fits, and a standardized path model. Full details are given in the supplementary materials.",
]

ACK = "We thank ISRIC – World Soil Information for WoSIS, the European Commission Joint Research Centre for LUCAS 2018 topsoil data, K. Georgiou and co-authors for the open fraction synthesis, the WorldClim, SoilGrids, GLiM, Köppen–Geiger, CMIP6, and ISIMIP communities for open data, and Y. Huang and co-authors for detailed supplementary methods that made this comparison possible. **Funding:** [to be completed]. **Author contributions:** [to be completed]. **Competing interests:** The authors declare no competing interests. **Data and materials availability:** All input datasets are publicly available from the sources cited. Global maps (10′ and 30 arc sec), model objects, analysis code, and the numerical results underlying all figures will be deposited in a public repository [DOI to be assigned on acceptance]."

